// Client-scoped derivation of the `laasie` dataset — NOT a second copy.
//
// This inherits every model, relationship, metric and description from `laasie` and adds one
// thing: the row-level restriction. Anything added to `laasie` — a new growth metric, a new
// model — appears here automatically, so the internal and external views cannot drift.
//
// WHY A SEPARATE DATASET AT ALL:
// Holistics evaluates row-level permission at the dataset level, and an embed token can only
// supply VALUES to a permission the dataset already declares — it cannot create the filter
// itself. So a single shared dataset carrying `owner_group_scope` would restrict Laasie's own
// staff too, and every internal non-Admin user would need the scoping attribute provisioned
// as the "All" value type just to see the whole platform. Anyone missed would silently get an
// empty dashboard — no error, no signal.
//
// Splitting the restriction into this derivation makes the internal/external boundary a
// property of the model rather than of per-user setup:
//   laasie         → unscoped → internal reporting (laasie_growth_performance)
//   laasie_client  → scoped   → external portal    (laasie_owner_portal, laasie_portal)
//
// Column-level PII redaction is NOT here: it lives on the models (`guest_email_pii`,
// `stakeholder_email_pii`), so it applies to both datasets automatically and composes with,
// rather than duplicates, this restriction.
Dataset laasie_client = laasie.extend({
  label: 'Laasie Loyalty (Client-Scoped)'

  // Row-level scoping for the external owner portal: a hotel owner or management company
  // sees only its own properties and everything that hangs off them.
  //
  // ANCHORED ON THE OWNER GROUP, NOT ON A FACT. The permission filters
  // `public_owner_groups`, and because the dataset has exactly one active path from every
  // fact to that model — bookings > properties > owner_groups, with loyalty transactions and
  // redemptions reaching it through bookings — restricting the group restricts every fact
  // behind it. Anchoring on a fact instead would leave the dimensions (properties, guests,
  // external_stakeholders) unfiltered and readable in full through self-serve exploration.
  //
  // That last one matters: `public_external_stakeholders` is the directory of named contacts
  // at EVERY client. Scoping it through the owner group is what stops one hotel group from
  // listing another group's staff. A fact-anchored permission would not touch it at all.
  //
  // DEMO-GRADE ATTRIBUTE — MUST CHANGE BEFORE THIS CARRIES ANYTHING REAL.
  // Scoped on `company_id` because that is what already exists in this demo tenant, so the
  // portal can be demoed without an Admin creating an attribute first. This is the same
  // shortcut SCSI shipped on, before commit 20912f39 moved it to a dedicated attribute.
  //
  // `company_id` is ALREADY BOUND to Brainstorm APAC's company_access permission across
  // three datasets (brainstorm_adoption, brainstorm_cases, brainstorm_speakup) on an
  // unrelated id-space. A user provisioned for Brainstorm who reaches this dataset would
  // silently resolve to a different organisation's rows — no error, no signal. That is
  // acceptable for a controlled hospitality demo and NOT acceptable for anything real.
  // Concretely: do not demo Laasie and Brainstorm APAC in one session on the same login.
  //
  // UPGRADE PATH (exactly what SCSI did): create a dedicated `laasie_owner_group_id` Number
  // user attribute — Manage Users > any user > Attributes > "Add or manage User Attributes" —
  // then change `value:` below back to 'laasie_owner_group_id'. Nothing else has to move.
  //
  // Not enforced for Admins or Analysts with data source access.
  // NOTE: RLS-as-code must be enabled for this tenant, and the permission only takes effect
  // after publish. `company_id` already exists, so no Admin step is required to demo.
  permission owner_group_scope {
    field: r(public_owner_groups.owner_group_id)
    operator: 'matches_user_attribute'
    value: 'company_id'
  }
})
